# {{PLUGIN_NAME}} — {{ORG_NAME}}'s agent harness, as an opencode configuration

This directory *is* the configuration. It ships the same harness {{ORG_NAME}} runs
elsewhere — the verbs, the agent contracts, the gates — packaged for **opencode**.

> **Owned by {{ORG_NAME}}.** It is generated, but it is yours: identity, version and
> distribution are the org's, and nothing here is a runtime dependency on a generator.

## Install

**Globally** (every project on this machine):

```
cp -R ./ ~/.config/opencode/
```

**Or per repo** (checked in, so a team shares one harness):

```
mkdir -p <repo>/.opencode && cp -R ./ <repo>/.opencode/
```

Either way the layout is the same:

```
opencode.json    model + the one-provider allowlist + the `task` deny
AGENTS.md        session preamble (points at the wiki; run /{{VERB_PRIME}} first)
command/         {{VERB_INTRO}} · {{VERB_SETUP}} · {{VERB_PRIME}} · {{VERB_INCEPTION}} · {{VERB_REFINE}} · {{VERB_EXECUTE}} · {{VERB_WIKI}} · {{VERB_HANDOFF}}
agent/           {{OC_IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} · {{OC_REVIEWER_AGENT}} · {{OC_CLEARANCE_AGENT}} (the roles `dispatch` runs, one file per name)
skill/           the procedure each command reads and follows
plugin/          dispatch.js — the orchestrator's one primitive (below); reminders.js — advisory nudges
```

**Restart opencode after copying.** Config, agents, commands and plugins are read at
startup — an already-running session will not see them.

## Authenticate

No credentials live here. Log the provider in once with opencode itself:

```
opencode auth login      # pick {{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}
```

If `{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}` needs provider options (a region, a profile, a base URL), add
them under `provider.{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}.options` in your own `opencode.json` — that is an
opencode setting, not part of this harness. Default model: `{{OC_DEFAULT_MODEL_REF}}`.

## One provider

The load-bearing control is the **allowlist**: `opencode.json` sets

```
"enabled_providers": ["{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}"]
```

so `/models` lists **only** `{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}` — including on a machine that already
has an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` or `OPENAI_API_KEY` in its environment, which opencode would
otherwise auto-detect as extra providers. `"disabled_providers": ["opencode"]` is kept
as belt-and-suspenders (it names the built-in Zen provider explicitly), but a deny-list
alone does **not** cover a provider you did not think to name — the allowlist does. The
`dispatch` tool enforces the same provider and the org's banned list per task.

## The read-only boundary

`agent/{{OC_REVIEWER_AGENT}}.md` denies `{{OC_REVIEWER_DENY_LIST}}`; `agent/{{OC_CLEARANCE_AGENT}}.md`
denies `{{OC_CLEARANCE_DENY_LIST}}`. A denied permission removes the tool from the model's
toolset *and* refuses at execution — so a validating agent cannot edit code, run a
command, or spawn a writer to do it for them. `{{OC_IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}}` deliberately has
no `permission` block: it needs full tools to do the work.

Each deny set is **derived at emit time** from that agent's own read-only allow-list in
the org config (deny = the dangerous capabilities it does not allow), so the config is
what decides the artifact — not a hardcoded list in a template.

**The filename is the contract.** `dispatch` runs an agent by file name, and a name that
does not resolve falls back to the full-permission primary agent (`{{OC_PRIMARY_AGENT}}`)
— which would silently run a "read-only" gate with edit/bash/task allowed. So these files
are named after the exact tokens the skills dispatch; renaming one by hand breaks the
boundary. Rename in the org config and re-emit instead.

## `dispatch` — commands to troops, not prompts

The orchestrator (your session) sends **commands**: `dispatch({ role, ticket, repo?,
model?, task_id?, command?, background? })`. The ticket is the whole envelope — the
role reads it from the tracker. Several calls in one turn run in parallel; `task_id`
continues the same troop (the feedback loop); `model` picks the model for that task
inside the org policy (one provider, banned list). Writers get a worktree in `repo`
under this workspace, so troops work across many repos at once; readers run in place.
Troops are remembered in `.worktrees/.dispatch.json`, so a restart keeps `task_id`
continuation working. When a ticket is done, remove its tree:
`git -C <repo> worktree remove <workspace>/.worktrees/<repo>--<ticket>`.

Read-only roles run bash only for the tracker's and git's read commands; opencode
matches the whole command text, so output redirection (`>`) and `--output` are denied
explicitly after the allows.
The built-in `task` is denied in `opencode.json`, and the read-only roles deny
`dispatch`, so a reviewer cannot launder a write through a troop.

## `plugin/reminders.js` — advisory, never blocking

Two nudges, both fail-open: a toast when a top-level session starts (run
`/{{VERB_PRIME}}`), and one line pushed into the compaction summary so a cycled session
knows the durable state lives in the tracker and `{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}`. Troop sessions are
not nudged.

